# kicad-mcp — AI working rules

- Target is **KiCad 10**. Start via `kicad-10` (`/usr/bin/kicad-10` from the .deb, or `scripts/kicad-10.sh`), never the `.AppImage` and never system KiCad 9. Preferences → Plugins → Enable IPC API. `board_summary` must show `10.x` and `net_ipc_persists: true`. If it shows 9.x, tell the user to start `kicad-10` — do not assign nets in the GUI.
- Only these tools exist. Read: `board_summary`, `get_footprints`, `get_nets`, `get_pads`, `check_placement`, `get_routing_scene`, `list_parts`, `get_part_pins`, `check_board`. Write: `download_lcsc_part`, `make_wire_pad`, `make_mounting_hole`, `place_footprint`, `place_parts`, `place_matrix`, `move_footprint`, `remove_footprint`, `clear_board`, `clear_zones`, `set_board_outline`, `connect_pins`, `connect_many`, `disconnect_pin`, `disconnect_many`, `add_track`, `add_tracks`, `add_via`, `add_vias`, `stitch_via`, `set_copper_zone`, `set_copper_layers`, `autoroute_nets`, `ripup_wire`, `check_drc`, `render_board`, `save_board`, `export_manufacturing`.
- Never create, edit, or parse `.kicad_pcb` / `.kicad_sch` by hand — use MCP / the KiCad GUI.
- Start with `board_summary`. After a batch of changes, `check_board` before reporting done. After copper, also `check_drc`.
- Write tools need `--allow-ai-write`.
- Coordinates are millimetres in KiCad's native origin (+x right, +y up). `list_parts` gives the template names `place_footprint` wants. Board size is Edge.Cuts (`set_board_outline`); there is no separate width/height field. Rectangle: omit origin to centre on the A4 drawing sheet. Polygon: `points: [{x_mm, y_mm}, ...]`. `set_board_outline` **replaces** existing Edge.Cuts by default (`replace=false` to add) and refills copper zones.
- Place parts on free space inside the board using F.CrtYd courtyards. Never stack footprints on the same spot or on an Edge.Cuts corner. Placement refuses courtyard overlap.
- LCSC parts must come from `download_lcsc_part` (EasyEDA geometry + pin names). Do not substitute a generic KiCad library footprint for a C-number. Wire pads and mounting holes are generated parametrically: `list_parts` writes the defaults (`WirePad_PTH` 2.5/1.5 mm, `MountingHole_M3_NPTH` 3.2 mm); `make_wire_pad` / `make_mounting_hole` write any other size (e.g. `WirePad_PTH_3.2_2`). Already placed footprints keep their geometry — remove and re-place to pick up a new size.
- **Pin names and functions come from EasyEDA.** `download_lcsc_part` returns `pins: [{number, pin_name}]`. For an already downloaded template call `get_part_pins`. Use those `pin_name` values for `connect_many`. Never invent nets from memory or Alladin `pad_nets` array order. A manufacturer datasheet is allowed **only after a logic check** shows EasyEDA cannot be right. Then fetch hard facts, name the contradiction, and net from the datasheet. Passives whose EasyEDA names are only `1`/`2` have no polarity — pick GND vs rail from the companion pad geometry.
- `connect_pins` / `connect_many` only assign nets (ratsnest). `disconnect_pin` / `disconnect_many` put a pin back on unconnected (same pad splice; every same-number pad). Every pad that shares a pin number is assigned (thermal clusters). Copper is `add_track` / `add_tracks` / `add_via` / `add_vias` / `stitch_via` / `set_copper_zone` / `set_copper_layers` / `autoroute_nets`. `set_copper_layers` sets 2/4/6/8 copper layers (not undoable). `set_copper_zone` pours on F.Cu / In1.Cu / In2.Cu / B.Cu; pads connect solid unless `thermal=true` (PTH 1.2 mm spokes; vias and SMD stay solid). `stitch_via` places a via + stub next to a pin (`reference`+`pin`) or every SMD pad on a net (`net: "GND"` or `"5V"`); it refuses pads and tracks, including a stub that would cross a data line. `autoroute_nets` needs an explicit `nets` list (never `*`). Refuse GND/VSS (pour a zone). USB_DN and USB_DP must be passed together (two singles + length-match). Optional `track_width_mm` / `via_size_mm` / `via_drill_mm` / `clearance_mm`; defaults pin 0.2 mm clearance and 0.6/0.3 vias. After reload, zones are refilled. Use it only when the human wants autorouting or a full A–Z board including copper. Do not autoroute unused GPIOs. 5V only if named. After autoroute, `check_board` and `check_drc`. That step writes the file via the Routing Tools CLI and reloads — no Ctrl+Z. Needs `kicad-routing-tools-setup`. `ripup_wire` takes `segment_id` from `get_routing_scene`. Do not send parallel copper writes — KiCad `BeginCommit` races.
- `save_board` only when the human asks. `autoroute_nets` and `check_drc` save internally. `export_manufacturing` saves, then writes JLCPCB files via `kicad-cli`: `<stem>_gerbers.zip`, `<stem>_bom.csv`, `<stem>_cpl.csv`. Silk has no U1/C3 refdes (JLCPCB DFM). Needs `kicad-cli` (KiCad package).
- Every write lands on KiCad's undo stack (Ctrl+Z).

## How this MCP builds a board (do not regress)

The pink A4 frame is the **drawing sheet**, not the PCB. The PCB is **Edge.Cuts**. That tool **replaces** existing Edge.Cuts unless `replace=false`. KiCad IPC reports the layer as `BL_Edge_Cuts`; replacement matches that name (and layer id 47).

`move_footprint` relocates/rotates a placed part in one undo: rigid transform of anchor + every baked pad — nets, reference and padstack geometry survive; the target is courtyard-checked. Copper does **not** move (re-route tracks to the part). `get_pads` is the verification tool: baked pad truth (reference, pin, net, absolute x/y, size, rotation, smd/pth/npth, layer, drill), filterable by `reference`/`net` — a mirrored or mis-rotated part shows pads on the wrong side of the anchor. `check_placement` is the hard audit: template pads recomputed at anchor+rotation vs the baked board pads; mirrored/mis-rotated/stale-baked parts fail with per-pad mm deltas, no-template parts are `skipped` (optional `reference`, `tolerance_mm` 0.01) — run it after placing or moving, trust it over any render. `render_board` refills zones, saves, and writes `<stem>_render_<side>.png` via `kicad-cli pcb render`; copper/silk/mask/holes are real but EasyEDA parts have no 3D bodies — package orientation comes from `get_pads`, not the render. `clear_board` deletes footprints, tracks, vias and zones; Edge.Cuts stays.

KiCad `CreateItems` stores `FootprintInstance.position` (what `get_footprints` reports) but draws nested pads at their raw coordinates. `place.rs` **bakes board millimetres into every pad**. If that bake is removed, copper piles up at the page origin (0,0 / top-left) while the API still claims the part is in the middle.

`connect_pins` / `connect_many` splice `Pad.net` (protobuf field 4) into the parent footprint and `UpdateItems` it — do not round-trip the whole padstack. `disconnect_pin` splices the same field to unconnected (code 0). A free pad UpdateItems is rejected. Every pad that shares the pin number is assigned. On KiCad 10 the net **persists**. After `connect_many`, `get_nets` / `check_board` must show the names.

After a `.deb` install, `.cursor/mcp.json` must launch `/usr/bin/kicad-mcp`. From a source tree: the **built binary** (`target/release/kicad-mcp` or `target/debug/kicad-mcp`), not `cargo run`. After Rust changes: rebuild, then toggle the MCP server off/on.
